{
  "dom_id": "step-q21273",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14221452/assets/399578c2a4d43e64be3f811ab0fdf0f5a62456c29d77dc3a822cc0ecea733c77.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2},\\,y=2t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $0\\le t\\le 1.$",
  "worked_solution": "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2t(4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\,|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
